this is the kokuma refugee camp in  northwestern kenya hundreds of thousands  of people live here but only one percent  of the population has access to  electricity from the main grid  instead most people rely on diesel power  generators that are expensive and  unreliable  vasco hamisi is changing that he's a  refugee from the democratic republic of  congo he knows how hard life can be  without access to affordable electricity  i decided to go in a green energy first  when i came here we are really  struggling  to get light  hemisi set up his company in 2017 and  secured funding from donors to build a  solar-powered mini grid  ocapi green energy now supplies  electricity to more than 200 businesses  including this shop run by another  refugee from the drc  with a reliable electricity supply i've  been able to buy a refrigerator i sell  cold drinks water and fresh mango juice  we're very grateful because this power  really helps us businesses pay hermesi's  company around seven dollars a month  that's about half the amount other  operators charge but demand is  outstripping supply i've seen that we  are still having more potential people  who are still coming to ask for  connection but for the power that we  have the 20 kilowatts that we have we  cannot connect everyone in kakuma plans  are underway to replicate the  solar-powered project in other parts of  the camp to build a brighter future for  the people living there  victoria gatenbee al jazeera
 

